Range-Based for Loop — 

A range-based for loop:

for(int x : arr) {
    cout << x;
}

works when arr is a range/iterable object whose beginning and ending can be determined.

Example 1 — Normal array: WORKS
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4};

for(int x : arr) {
    cout << x;
}

Here C++ knows the array's size because arr is an actual array in this scope.

Example 2 — Vector: WORKS
vector<int> arr = {1, 2, 3, 4};

for(int x : arr) {
    cout << x;
}

vector knows its own range, so this works directly.

Example 3 — Function parameter int arr[]: DOES NOT WORK
void print(int arr[]) {
    for(int x : arr) {   // ❌
        cout << x;
    }
}

Why?

Inside a function parameter, this:

int arr[]

is treated as:

int* arr

The function receives only a pointer to the first element. It does not automatically know the array's size.

So use:

void print(int arr[], int n) {
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i];
    }
}
IMPORTANT

Don't remember:

❌ “For-each requires a known array size.”

Remember:

✅ “Range-based for needs something iterable. A raw array works when its array information is still available, but an array passed to a function as int arr[] decays to a pointer, so I need the size separately.”

Why did LeetCode sometimes let me use for-each?

Because LeetCode often gives you:

vector<int>& arr

A vector knows its own size/range:

for(int x : arr) {
    ...
}

So there is no problem.

